* Hello World, with the counted runs written as the numbers they are.
*
* This expands to programs/basic/hello_world.bf character for character, which
* is what makes it a test as well as an example: the same program, said in a
* way you can check, against one whose output the manifest already pins down.
*
* Nothing here is a new abstraction yet -- no variables, no macros. It is the
* smallest thing that shows the point of the preprocessor: a run of eight '+'
* is a number, and reads better as one.
*
* The setup, which is the only part with a loop in it:
*
*     tape[0] = 8
*     while tape[0]:           # eight rounds, filling four cells as it goes
*         tape[1] += 4
*         tape[2] += 7
*         tape[3] += 3
*         tape[4] += 1
*         tape[0] -= 1
*
* After which every letter is that row nudged up or down to the character
* wanted, which is what the rest of the file is.

* --- setup: eight rounds of a loop that fills four cells -------------------

@define SEED   8    * outer counter, in cell 0
@define ROW    4    * inner counter, and the step back to it

@define FILL_A 2    * what each round adds to the four cells it fills
@define FILL_B 3
@define FILL_C 3
@define FILL_D 1

+{SEED}[>+{ROW}[>+{FILL_A}>+{FILL_B}>+{FILL_C}>+{FILL_D}<{ROW}-]>+>+>->>+[<]<-]

* --- print: each adjustment named for the letter it lands on ---------------

@define E_DOWN    3
@define L_UP      7
@define O_UP      3
@define W_DOWN    1
@define R_UP      3
@define L_DOWN    6
@define D_DOWN    8
@define BANG_UP   1
@define NEWLINE   2

>>.               * H
>-{E_DOWN}.       * e
+{L_UP}..         * l l
+{O_UP}.          * o
>>.               * space
<-{W_DOWN}.       * W
<.                * o
+{R_UP}.          * r
-{L_DOWN}.        * l
-{D_DOWN}.        * d
>>+{BANG_UP}.     * !
>+{NEWLINE}.      * newline
